@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Accessibility)]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Page_Title_Overview_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<div class="info-box info-box--coloured">
    <h1>@GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Accessibility</h1>

    <p>
        The goal of the Blazor Ramp project is to provide free, open-source, accessibility-first components for your Blazor project, making the end result more inclusive.
    </p>

    <p>
        Each component is manually tested using the following Assistive Technology (AT) and browser combinations. I strive to ensure that the components work
        with the widest combination set possible whilst still providing components that meet both the developers' and end users' expectations.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="info-box">

    <h2 id="screen-reader-testing" class="info-box__heading">Screen reader testing</h2>

    <ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-testing">
        <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows are each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
        <li>VoiceOver on macOS is paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
        <li>VoiceOver on iPhone is paired with mobile Safari.</li>
        <li>Talkback on Android is paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        I neither have access to and/or the finances for many other devices, but would like to do more with Voice Control.
        As I am on Windows that has built in Voice access I can at lease ensure that the voice commands work as expected i.e they activate
        the correct item. The Voice Control software will assign numbers to items when there is more than one actionable item using the accessible name.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="info-box">

    <h2 class="info-box__heading">Approach to accessibility</h2>

    <p>
        Although Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are extremely important, I take the approach of first making the components usable for AT users and then worrying about
        meeting specific WCAG requirements.
    </p>
    <p>
        I neither have access to nor the finances for many other devices, but would like to do more with Voice Control. As I am on Windows, which has built-in Voice Access, I can at
        least ensure that the voice commands work as expected, i.e. they activate the correct item. The Voice Control software will assign numbers to items when there is more than one
        actionable item, using the accessible name.
    </p>
</section>

<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Left_Arrow_Modifier" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Getting_Started">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_About - @GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Getting_Started</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Right_Arrow_Modifier" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Roadmap">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_About - @GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Roadmap</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
</div>